-improving-sprinting-performance-in-football-players.pdf","sourceTitle":"The Need for Speed—Improving Sprinting Performance in Football Players","sourcePublisher":"National Strength and Conditioning Association","sourceKind":"professional_standard","evidenceQuality":84,"claims":["The athlete should accelerate progressively into upright high-speed mechanics rather than attack the fly zone from a cold or abrupt start.","Use a small number of observable cues: build smoothly, run tall without leaning back, strike beneath the body, keep arm action coordinated, sprint through the zone, and decelerate gradually."]},{"sectionKey":"safety_stop_rules","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9414047/","sourceTitle":"The Influence of Weekly Sprint Volume and Maximal Velocity Exposures on Eccentric Hamstring Strength in Professional Football Players","sourcePublisher":"Sports","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":84,"claims":["High-speed sprint load and recent exposure must be monitored rather than inferred from repetition count alone.","Stop for pain, hamstring or calf tightness, limping, stumble, unsafe surface or spacing, repeated overstriding, loss of upright control, or a persistent velocity or timing decline."]},{"sectionKey":"programming","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6872694/","sourceTitle":"The Training and Development of Elite Sprint Performance: an Integration of Scientific and Best Practice Literature","sourcePublisher":"Sports Medicine - Open","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":90,"claims":["Flying sprints are used to develop maximal velocity and should continue only while velocity and mechanics remain stable.","Flying Sprint belongs early in a session after preparation and before fatigue-oriented work; rehabilitation, conditioning, and assisted-speed uses require separate contextual profiles."]},{"sectionKey":"athlete_support","sourceUrl":"https://worldathletics.org/download/downloadnsa?f